WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.780
Threat intelligence is only useful when it reaches the right consumer in a form they can

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:04.048
use.

00:00:04.048 --> 00:00:08.315
A chief information security officer does not need the same output as a detection

00:00:08.315 --> 00:00:08.720
engineer.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.015
A SOC analyst does not need the same language as a board risk committee.

00:00:12.015 --> 00:00:16.240
A vulnerability manager, incident responder, fraud analyst, platform engineer,

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.830
and executive leader may all care about the same threat,

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.800
but each has a different decision to make.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.100
Strategic intelligence supports leaders, risk owners, and planners.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.302
It answers questions about threat trends, business exposure, geopolitical developments,

00:00:28.302 --> 00:00:31.960
sector risk, likely adversary interest, and investment priorities.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.075
Strategic products are usually written in business and risk language.

00:00:35.075 --> 00:00:39.020
They should avoid unnecessary technical detail while preserving analytic confidence and

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:39.920
key assumptions.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.700
A good strategic brief helps leaders decide where to allocate attention, budget,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.795
and accountability.

00:00:44.795 --> 00:00:49.240
Operational intelligence supports security operations, incident response, threat hunting,

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.450
and program planning.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:55.180
It explains campaigns, adversary objectives, affected sectors, likely intrusion patterns,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.815
and defensive implications.

00:00:56.815 --> 00:00:59.735
Operational CTI can help a SOC prepare triage guidance,

00:00:59.735 --> 00:01:04.265
an incident response team scope an investigation, or a hunting team choose a hypothesis.

00:01:04.265 --> 00:01:08.360
It often sits between high-level risk reporting and highly specific detection data.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.145
Operational products should be specific enough to influence a workflow but not so narrow

00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:14.640
that they become a pile of disconnected artifacts.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.250
For example, a short campaign note might describe observed phishing themes,

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.480
likely targets, affected technologies, expected telemetry, recommended searches,

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.780
and escalation criteria.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.760
That product can help SOC analysts, incident responders,

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.595
and hunters coordinate around the same threat picture without requiring every reader to

00:01:30.595 --> 00:01:32.205
parse the original source material.

00:01:32.205 --> 00:01:35.960
Tactical and technical intelligence supports day-to-day defensive action.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.630
Tactical intelligence describes adversary behaviors,

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:43.060
such as common initial access methods, persistence approaches, lateral movement patterns,

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.280
or data staging behavior.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.790
Technical intelligence includes observables such as IP addresses, domains, URLs,

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:53.500
file hashes, email artifacts, registry paths, process names, certificate details,

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.360
and malware configuration values.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.480
These details can enrich alerts, seed detections, support triage,

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.340
or guide blocking decisions when they are current and relevant.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.920
The same source can produce different intelligence products.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.750
A malware report might become an executive note about risk to a business unit,

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.540
a SOC bulletin with triage cues, a hunting hypothesis,

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.315
a detection engineering ticket, and a short list of indicators for enrichment.

00:02:14.315 --> 00:02:16.860
The analyst's job is not to forward everything to everyone.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.370
The job is to understand the audience, the decision, the time sensitivity,

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.480
and the level of detail needed for action.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.070
When in doubt, name the consumer and the intended action at the top of the work.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:30.070
"For SOC triage" leads to a different product than "for quarterly risk review." This small

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.840
habit prevents mismatched outputs,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:32.770
reduces rework,

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.870
and helps the CTI team show how intelligence supports real defensive decisions.
